<<<
codlin(g). Spell with the g.
===
**codlin(g).** Spell with the g.
>>>

<<<
c(o)enobite. Spell coe-; see Æ, Œ.
===
**c(o)enobite.** Spell coe-; see ^^Æ, Œ^^.
>>>

<<<
coercible, -eable. Spell -ible; see -ABLE 2,
===
**coercible, -eable.** Spell *-ible*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
coffee. Pronounce k6-, not kaw-.
===
**coffee.** Pronounce kŏ-, not kaw-.
>>>

<<<
cog. The phrase cogged dice is due to a misunderstanding of the old io cog dice, which meant not to load them, but to cheat in throwing them; loaded should be used.
===
**cog.** The phrase *cogged dice* is due to a misunderstanding of the old *to cog dice,* which meant not to load them, but to cheat in throwing them; *loaded* should be used.
>>>

<<<
cogent. See -ER & -EST, 4.
===
**cogent.** See ^^-ER & -EST^^, 4.
>>>

<<<
cogitate makes -itable; see -ABLE 1.
===
**cogitate** makes *-itable*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1.
>>>

<<<
cognate. For the sense in grammar see TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**cognate.** For the sense in grammar see ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
cognizance, cognizant, cognizable, cognize. Cognize alone has the -galways sounded. Of the four, cognizance is the word from which the others have sprung, & it had for some time no -g- to be sounded;
===
**cognizance, cognizant, cognizable, cognize.** *Cognize* alone has the *-g-* always sounded. Of the four, *cognizance* is the word from which the others have sprung, & it had for some time no *-g-* to be sounded;
>>>

<<<
the introduction of the -g- has affected pronunciation, but kòn- is still common in the first three, & should be maintained at least in cognizance & cognizant;
===
the introduction of the *-g-* has affected pronunciation, but kŏn- is still common in the first three, & should be maintained at least in *cognizance* & *cognizant*;
>>>

<<<
cognizable should be either k6’nizabl or, if it is to be assimilated to cognize, kégni’zabl. For synonyms of -nce, see SIGN.
===
*cognizable* should be either kŏ'nĭzabl or, if it is to be assimilated to *cognize,* kŏgnī'zabl. For synonyms of *-nce,* see ^^SIGN^^.
>>>

<<<
coiffeur, coiffure. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**coiffeur, coiffure.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
coincidence. The long arm of c. is a HACKNEYED PHRASE.
===
**coincidence.** *The long arm of c.* is a ^^HACKNEYED PHRASE^^.
>>>

<<<
worse:—The author does not strain
===
worse:—*The author does not strain
>>>

<<<
garden a little too heavily. colander, cullender.
===
garden a little too heavily.*

**colander, cullender.**
>>>

<<<
Both are pronounced kii’lender; the first spelling, which is nearer the Latin stem (cf. percolate), is also more frequent in the 19th-c. quotations in the OED. col-, com-, con-. There is a tendency
===
Both are pronounced kŭ'lender; the first spelling, which is nearer the Latin stem (cf. *percolate*), is also more frequent in the 19th-c. quotations in the OED.

**col-, com-, con-.** There is a tendency
>>>

<<<
(such as collective, colloquial, combustible, commandment, commercial, concomitant, condition, confederate) with a distinct short o
===
(such as *collective, colloquial, combustible, commandment, commercial, concomitant, condition, confederate*) with a distinct short o
>>>

<<<
(kéndi’shn, kémah’ndment, instead of the correct kndi’shn, komah‘ndmnt).
===
(kŏndĭ'shn, kŏmah'ndment, instead of the correct kndĭ'shn, komah'ndmnt).
>>>

<<<
colicky. Spell the adj. with -cky; cf. bivouac(ked), & -c-, -cK-.
===
**colicky.** Spell the adj. with *-cky*; cf. *bivouac(ked),* & -c-, -cK-.
>>>

<<<
collapsable, -ible. The first is better; see -ABLE 2.
===
**collapsable, -ible.** The first is better; see ^^-ABLE^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
collate. See -aATABLE.
===
**collate.** See ^^-ATABLE^^.
>>>

<<<
collation. The reporter who can be content with repast instead wins the moderate gratitude, & he who says meal the fervent admiration, of most of us; see WORKING & STYLISH WORDS.
===
**collation.** The reporter who can be content with *repast* instead wins the moderate gratitude, & he who says *meal* the fervent admiration, of most of us; see ^^WORKING & STYLISH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
collectable, -ible. The first is better; see -ABLE 2.
===
**collectable, -ible.** The first is better; see ^^-ABLE^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
COLLECTIVES. The word is applied to many different things, & in TECHNICAL TERMS an attempt is made to disentangle them. Flock! (a number of sheep or parishioners) is a collective of one kind, & flock? (woollen waste) is of another;
===
**COLLECTIVES.** The word is applied to many different things, & in ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^ an attempt is made to disentangle them. *Flock* 1 (a number of sheep or parishioners) is a collective of one kind, & *flock* 2 (woollen waste) is of another;
>>>

<<<
flock * may be treated as singular or plural (His f. was attacked by wolves; His f. was without a pastor or were unanimous in disapproval), & can itself be used in the plural with the ordinary difference in meaning from the singular (shepherds tending their flocks);
===
*flock* 1 may be treated as singular or plural (*His f. was attacked by wolves; His f. was without a pastor or were unanimous in disapproval*), & can itself be used in the plural with the ordinary difference in meaning from the singular (*shepherds tending their flocks*);
>>>

<<<
flock? can be used either in the singular or in the plural, the meaning being the same, a material, & flock being treated always as singular & flocks as plural (A flock matiress; A mattress of flock or flocks; the flock has, the fiocks have, not been disinfected).
===
*flock* 2 can be used either in the singular or in the plural, the meaning being the same, a material, & *flock* being treated always as singular & *flocks* as plural (*A flock mattress; A mattress of flock or flocks; the flock has, the flocks have, not been disinfected*).
>>>

<<<
But the word collective is applied to both, as well as to many equally dissimilar kinds of noun, for which see TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
But the word *collective* is applied to both, as well as to many equally dissimilar kinds of noun, for which see ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
1. Words that have no separate plural form, but are the same in both
===
**1.** Words that have no separate plural form, but are the same in both
>>>

<<<
f one kind, & *flock* 2
===
f one kind, & *flock*²
>>>

<<<
s of another; *flock* 1
===
s of another; *flock*¹
>>>

<<<
eir flocks*); *flock* 2
===
eir flocks*); *flock*²
